officials say the alleged gunman in  Buffalo's mass shooting chose his Target  because of the high percentage of black  people in that particular area which  raises a question how did that  neighborhood become so segregated in the  first place a University of Michigan  analysis found that the Buffalo Niagara  Falls metro area overall is the sixth  most segregated in the whole U.S and  another study by the University of  Buffalo concluded last year that the  economic conditions for black people in  that City had not improved in three  decades we spoke to Professor Henry  Lewis Taylor who worked on that study  and who told us why Buffalo's east side  looks the way it does today  the process of segregation is driven by  the way in which we build cities within  the urban areas ever since the rise of  home ownership in the United States  there have been a sorting and sifting  process to separate higher income whites  from lower income blacks back in 1940  Buffalo was one of the most integrated  cities in the United States but as home  ownership increased and as we saw this  land valuation system occurred blacks  were concentrated in places on the east  side where the most undesirable  residential lands in the city existed  and is there a connection between that  history and what we saw unfold this past  weekend I think it's a big connection it  if you drive around this neighborhood  and community and you see folks living  in the dilapidated and run down houses  these streets and sidewalks that are  literally non-existent the unkept vacant  Lots  black people fight against this so  because the people in the neighborhood  like this fight against the condition of  the neighborhood it makes them a Target  it makes them a Target  I take it you were not surprised or  already knew that this was the only full  service grocery in this area there are  over a hundred thousand black people in  Buffalo and the fact that there's only  one major Supermarket servicing them is  a disgrace  but that was years of struggle and and  battle and fighting it just didn't  happen and so it it represents a symbol  of Hope and possibility it represents  what we can accomplish When we struggle  just to get one food store so that's  what this food shop one of the few  places where we can get nutritionist  food to eat and you attack it  you attack it  you're the anguish in his voice  Professor Taylor is incredibly  knowledgeable uh and passionate speaker  his point is that when you look at the  built environment around you remember  it's it's not an accident that it looks  the way it does and it's not a choice  either it's deliberate yeah just like  this attack was deliberate well done  Tony
 

